#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""visualize-phone-data — pull device/GPS data + GPS exclusion zones off an
attached XenoMetaRadar phone and visualize them on OpenStreetMap.

The app stores, on the phone (app-private storage of the debug build
``com.darkmentor.debug``):

  * databases/app-database          Room SQLite DB with the `device`,
                                    `location` and `device_to_location` tables.
  * shared_prefs/app-prefs.xml      SharedPreferences; the GPS exclusion zones
                                    live under the `key_exclusion_zones` key as
                                    a JSON array of {circle|square} objects.

This script copies those files off the phone *read-only* (``adb exec-out
run-as <pkg> cat ...`` — it never writes to or wipes the device), reads them
locally, then serves a single-page Leaflet/OpenStreetMap visualization from a
local web server and opens it in your browser. Two views:

  * "Raw data"                 — every GPS coordinate any device was seen at,
                                 with the exclusion zones drawn as DOTTED
                                 outlines (no fill).
  * "Data after GPS exclusions"— the same map but every device that *ever* had
                                 a coordinate inside any exclusion zone is
                                 removed entirely; zones drawn as SOLID lines.

Usage:
    ./visualize-phone-data                       # auto-pick the only device
    ./visualize-phone-data -s 1234ABCD     # choose a device by serial
    ./visualize-phone-data -s P1                 # ...or by alias (device-aliases.json)
    ./visualize-phone-data --no-serve --out viz.html   # just build the HTML
    ./visualize-phone-data --db app-database --prefs app-prefs.xml  # offline

Device aliases: put a JSON map of friendly-name -> serial in `device-aliases.json`
next to this script, e.g. {"P1": "1234ABCD", "P2": "5678WXYZ"}. Then
`-s P1` resolves to that serial (matching is case-insensitive).

Requires: python3 (stdlib only), adb on PATH, and internet access (for the
OpenStreetMap map tiles + the Leaflet library from a CDN).
"""

